{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>לְאֵיתָנוֹ.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Shemot Rabbah 21:6): ״לִתְנָאוֹ הָרִאשׁוֹן״ — to its earlier stipulation. And it is difficult for me according to their words: this is not the place of the stipulation, but rather at the time of the division there it should mention this stipulation. Or perhaps, when the verse informs us that after it returned, then it knew that it was a stipulation that HaShem had stipulated with it, and not a decree of its annihilation or the termination of its existence to abolish it from the world. And although the stipulation was already known to it, perhaps — in order to show the wondrousness of the matter, that it dried up and the waters became dry land, and also the standing waters became walls of stone — one may say that when it reached its extreme it too [thought it was permanent], and when it returned it knew that this was only the original stipulation alone. Now one needs to know the nature of the stipulation: if it was for those who came out of Egypt, if so, why did it protest at this time, as they said of blessed memory (ibid.) that it defied Moses and said to him: ״I will not be split for your sake, for I was created on the third day and you were created on the sixth״ etc., until HaShem inclined His right hand to the right of Moses, as it is written (Isaiah 63:12): ״מוֹלִיךְ לִימִין מֹשֶׁה.״ And furthermore, I see that for singled-out ones of the treasure it was split against its will, as in the episode brought in Chullin (Chullin 7a) regarding Rabbi Pinchas ben Yair etc.; and if He had stipulated with it only for those who came out of Egypt alone, what power did Rabbi Pinchas ben Yair, peace be upon him, have to prevail over the works of Creation?" "<b>אָכֵן</b> this stipulation is included among the conditions that HaShem stipulated over all the work of Creation — to be subject to the Torah and its laborers, and to do all that they decree upon them, with their dominion over them like the dominion of the Creator, blessed be He. And therefore you will find likewise in the heavens and the earth and the stars and the sun and the moon, that the singular righteous ones ruled over them, and all the more so the multitudes, as HaShem decreed for them at the time of Creation. And this is the sod of what He said (Isaiah 43:1): ״בּוֹרַאֲךָ יַעֲקֹב״ etc., and they said of blessed memory (Vayikra Rabbah 36:4) that the Holy One, blessed be He, said to His world: Who created you, who formed you — Israel, and all is through the power of the Torah. Go forth and learn from what I have written on the verse in Genesis." "Now behold, at Israel's exodus from Egypt they had not yet received the Torah, and His decree over the creations was not [yet] a decree, and therefore the sea did not agree to split for them, and it argued to Moses: ״You were created on the sixth [day] and I on the third.״ This is a hint that he [Moses] is not a son of Torah, for if he were a son of Torah, behold he preceded it, for the Torah preceded the entire world. And therefore HaShem acted wisely and led His right hand to the right of Moses — meaning, He showed him that he is a son of Torah who traces his lineage to it as the right [side], as it is written (Deuteronomy 33:2): ״מִימִינוֹ״ etc., and when it saw this, immediately and forthwith it was split according to the original stipulation. And therefore every single righteous one who stands after the giving of the Torah brings in his hand one writ of indebtedness to force it to split before him. And you will find that when it did not wish to split for Rabbi Pinchas ben Yair and for his attendant, it wished to penalize him, and the sea was afraid of him." "<b>וּמִצְרַיִם נָסִים</b> etc. — meaning, that although they were fleeing from it when they saw that it had returned לְאֵיתָנוֹ, nevertheless ״לִקְרָאתוֹ״ — meaning, the sea was coming toward them to the place where they were fleeing there, and to every place where they would flee they would find themselves heading toward it, after the manner of what they said of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 110a; Bamidbar Rabbah ch. 18) regarding the swallowing of Korah and his congregation, that the mouth of the earth ran after them to the place where they would stand there, for all the creations do the will of their Maker with great zeal and caution, and the verse's measure — that the sea prevailed לְאֵיתָנוֹ — means to its full strength, and it became as mentioned." "<b>וַיְנַעֵר ה׳.</b> — meaning, because there are people skilled at sailing the sea, even when the sea is in all its strength and might they devise ways to navigate it; therefore the verse says against these: ״וַיְנַעֵר״ etc. — He nullified that power from them. And according to the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Shemot Rabbah 21), who said that ״Mitzrayim״ — Mit is the name of the prince of Egypt, it says that He embodied him and brought the rain of the waters upon him and tossed him about within etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:28:24.069248612Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}